69:4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation writes `accepted` and `integrating` atomically: it sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, binds the revision to its **producer role and archetype**, and routes the element to `integrating` — accepted but not landed, and accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator routes a new tracked run carrying that exact producer role and archetype; that run — not the Orchestrator — runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and leaves the leaf `integrating`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` creates no commit and likewise stays `integrating`. `done` is produced only by the integration transaction, and `worktree checkpoint` or `worktree integrate` from a reviewer, an orchestrator, or any run whose role/archetype is not the bound producer's is refused. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.
71:4b. **`checkpoint` never closes anything.** A checkpoint makes no board transition at all: the leaf it commits stays `integrating`. When the leaf is the Story's last open child it is also its Story's integration unit, so `worktree checkpoint` refuses it with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch and is the only thing that makes the `done` transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.
73:A checkpointed leaf is closed by its Story's integration, not by a later status change. `worktree integrate` lands the Story branch, which carries every checkpoint commit on it, so the same transaction that makes the final leaf `done` also closes every sibling that is `integrating` with durable checkpoint evidence — and only those. A sibling accepted but never checkpointed has nothing on the branch to land: it keeps blocking, and the fix is to checkpoint it, not to close it. That is also why a checkpointed sibling does not hide the Story's final leaf: the leaf that would close the scope is computed against the leaves this landing will carry.
74:5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.
